WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.520
Claude just turned a single prompt into a video that explains recursion by switching styles nine times. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 178. The researcher typed one paragraph into Claude Opus 5.5, and a post on X walked through the result. Claude is an artificial intelligence or ai chatbot. A prompt is just the instructions you type in plain language. The ask was a radically different video style for every explanation.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.640
It also had to feel self-referential, clever, and fast-moving, all at once.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.080
Self-referential means the clip keeps pointing at itself while it talks.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.040
What came back was a finished clip that runs just a few minutes.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.580
Picture a classmate retelling one's science fact as a comic, then stop motion, then pixel art,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.320
without standing up. Recursion means an idea that points back at itself,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.520
like a nesting doll hiding a smaller copy inside.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.960
The model kept that looping idea in every section while the pictures kept changing outfits.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.720
It finished in one pass with no extra rounds of fixes for each new look.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:15.480
If you learn better from visuals, a math or science idea can become a short video that changes tone, so people actually watch.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.260
You can try a smaller version right now on the cloud website.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:27.040
Ask it to explain a school subject using three different visual approaches and notice how far one request can carry the look.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.220
Here is how the switching works.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.700
A group chat is a decent teacher here.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.860
You ask someone to tell the same joke as a pirate, then a robot, then a news anchor.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.760
The joke stays the joke, only the costume of the words changes.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.660
Now scale that request from a text thread to a whole video.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.180
First the model reads the full paragraph as one bundle of rules it has to satisfy together.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.520
Not a shopping list it ticks off later.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:58.400
here. Second, it assigns a look to each chunk before it draws anything, like old film here

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.020
and comic panels there. Third, it builds each scene to match those rules while the spoken

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.700
idea still loops back on itself. Fourth, it tracks the earlier choices the way you remember

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:15.560
which voice did the pirate bit, so a later section still nods to an earlier one. It is also

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:21.220
a bit like filming one story, then dropping a new TikTok filter on every scene. The plot

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.880
Not stays put, the vibe never sits still.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.220
Each new look is chosen on the fly, so the pictures do not go still.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.920
Yet the core explanation keeps pointing at earlier parts.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.220
That self-pointing quality has to survive every costume change.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.260
So a new section still admits the video is talking about itself.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.340
What feels handmade is really one paragraph of instructions being obeyed at the same time.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:52.260
Once you see it as rule following at scale, that crafted feeling makes a lot more sense.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.920
Chat GPT can now link to email accounts, a Reddit user reported.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.000
So you can ask it to message you on a future date.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.240
It writes the email, schedules it, and delivers it when you asked.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.940
That person hooked up both an Outlook inbox and a separate Gmail account through the built-in

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:10.380
connectors.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.140
Connectors are little permission bridges that let the chat bot reach your inbox.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:22.840
They tested it by asking the AI to email them one hour later, and the message lined up correctly for later delivery.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.680
Think of it as a sticky note for your future self, except the door it sticks to is your inbox.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.560
You do not need a secret reminder app for a nudge like this.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:38.780
Go to the chat GPT website, connect Outlook or Gmail through the connectors, then type this.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.080
Email me about my history project next Friday at 6 in the evening.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.000
Watch it set up the scheduled message, and notice that the send time is in the future, not right now.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.180
Another maker skipped drawing tools altogether.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:58.240
One creator built a whole animated story where every scene is drawn in React and SVG,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.820
which are tools that paint pictures with code instead of a brush.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:09.660
SVG is a format that describes shapes and lines as instructions, so a circle is a recipe, not a photograph.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:17.540
The music and sound effects were written in Python, a programming language that can compose audio the way a recipe composes a meal.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:26.800
The only files that are not code are a few hundred small textures, plus open source fonts which are typefaces anyone is allowed to use.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:35.100
Those textures include film grain, the speckled look of old movie film, and bokeh, those soft blurry light circles and photos.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.800
photos. They were generated ahead of time, so building the final video would go faster.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.180
The result looks like a polished short film, but it is actually all generated instructions.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.380
If you like animation, this is a peek at code standing in for pencils and paint. Try

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:57.040
this on the Claude website. Ask it to generate simple animated SVG code for a bouncing moon

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.560
over a city. Notice how the code describes shapes and motion instead of painting each frame

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:09.480
by hand. You can paste that code into a free in browser SVG editor and hit play to see the motion.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:16.180
Someone posted on X a short AI-made tennis clip with smooth player movement and crowd reactions.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:22.520
A few details still look off. The ball-path wobbles, the serve does not always clear the net,

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:29.140
and opponents sometimes celebrate a win in odd ways. Even so, the overall motion already feels close

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.980
to real footage, like a sports video game that almost passed for the match on TV.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.400
Over on Reddit, a user asked chat GPT to write the next season of a TV series they invented

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.820
themselves. The model kept the characters and tone consistent, and it remembered details

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:51.000
from earlier parts without being reminded. It is like a dungeon master who never forgets

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.680
your backstory, sitting beside you for the next chapter. And that's a wrap! If any of today's

